---
source: crates/trousseau-cli/tests/help.rs
expression: stdout
---
Create a new, empty store.

Without flags, creates `.trousseau` in the current directory, encrypted to your own key. If you have
no identity yet, `init` generates one and prints your recipient (your public key, `age1...`). Share
that recipient with teammates so they can add you to their stores.

Usage: trousseau init [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --recipient <R>
          A recipient (public key) to encrypt to: `age1...`, an SSH public key, or a plugin
          recipient. Repeatable

      --recipients-file <PATH>
          A file of recipients, one per line (blank lines and `#` comments ignored)

      --passphrase
          Create a passphrase store instead of a recipients store

      --no-self
          Do not add the caller's own recipient to a recipients store

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Global options:
      --store <PATH>
          Use this store file. Wins over `--global` and over store discovery
          
          [env: TROUSSEAU_STORE=]

      --global
          Use your personal store instead of the nearest project store (the `.trousseau` file in
          this directory or a parent)

      --identity <PATH>
          An identity (private key) file to try when unlocking. Repeatable. Also tried:
          `TROUSSEAU_IDENTITY_FILE`, the config file's `identity.files`, the default identity file,
          `~/.ssh/id_ed25519` and `~/.ssh/id_rsa`

      --passphrase-file <PATH>
          Read the store's passphrase from this file instead of prompting. One trailing newline is
          stripped

      --json
          Print one JSON document on stdout instead of text. Errors become a JSON object on stderr

      --quiet
          Suppress informational messages on stderr. Errors still print

      --no-input
          Never prompt. A command that would prompt fails instead: exit 4 for a secret, exit 2 for a
          confirmation

Examples:
  A project store in this directory, encrypted to your own key:
    trousseau init

  Your personal store, used when no project store is found:
    trousseau init --global

  A store shared with a teammate's age key and an SSH key:
    trousseau init --recipient age1... --recipient "ssh-ed25519 AAAA..."

  Recipients from a file, one per line ('#' comments allowed):
    trousseau init --recipients-file team.txt

  A store protected by a passphrase instead of keys:
    trousseau init --passphrase

Notes:
  init refuses to overwrite an existing store (exit 8).
  The store file is encrypted, so committing '.trousseau' to git is fine.
  Your identity file is the private half. Never commit or share it.
